Here's my analysis of `InputValidatorTests.cs`:

---

## Summary

**1 major finding** — your 9 test methods are textbook copy-paste tests that differ only in the input value and expected outcome. This is the exact pain you described: every new case requires a brand new method.

---

## Finding: Copy-Paste Tests → Parameterized Tests (Category 3)

All 9 methods follow an identical structure:

```csharp
var validator = new InputValidator();
var result = validator.Validate(INPUT);
Assert.Is_____(result.IsValid);
Assert.AreEqual(EXPECTED_MSG, result.ErrorMessage);
```

### Suggested refactoring — use `[DataRow]`

**Invalid-input cases** (lines 8–88, 6 methods → 1):

```csharp
[DataTestMethod]
[DataRow("", "Input cannot be empty", DisplayName = "Empty string")]
[DataRow(null, "Input cannot be empty", DisplayName = "Null")]
[DataRow("   ", "Input cannot be empty", DisplayName = "Whitespace only")]
[DataRow("ab", "Input must be at least 3 characters", DisplayName = "Too short")]
[DataRow("hello<script>", "Input contains invalid characters", DisplayName = "Special chars")]
public void Validate_InvalidInput_ReturnsFalse(string? input, string expectedError)
{
    var validator = new InputValidator();
    var result = validator.Validate(input);
    Assert.IsFalse(result.IsValid);
    Assert.AreEqual(expectedError, result.ErrorMessage);
}
```

For the "too long" case (which builds a string at runtime), use `[DynamicData]`:

```csharp
public static IEnumerable<object[]> TooLongData =>
    new[] { new object[] { new string('x', 256), "Input must not exceed 255 characters" } };

[DataTestMethod]
[DynamicData(nameof(TooLongData))]
public void Validate_TooLong_ReturnsFalse(string input, string expectedError) { /* same body */ }
```

**Valid-input cases** (3 methods → 1):

```csharp
[DataTestMethod]
[DataRow("hello-world", DisplayName = "Normal input")]
[DataRow("abc", DisplayName = "Min length boundary")]
public void Validate_ValidInput_ReturnsTrue(string input)
{
    var validator = new InputValidator();
    var result = validator.Validate(input);
    Assert.IsTrue(result.IsValid);
    Assert.IsNull(result.ErrorMessage);
}
```

(The max-length `new string('a', 255)` case also needs `[DynamicData]`.)

---

## Impact

| Metric | Before | After |
|--------|--------|-------|
| Methods | 9 | 3–4 |
| Lines | ~80 | ~40 |
| Adding a new case | New method (8 lines) | **One new `[DataRow]` attribute (1 line)** |

## Trade-offs

- **Gained:** Adding cases is now a single attribute line; less scrolling, less boilerplate.
- **Lost:** Individual methods are slightly more readable in isolation; `[DynamicData]` cases are less discoverable than inline `[DataRow]`.
- **Verdict:** Strongly worth it at 9 occurrences. This is the canonical use case for parameterized tests in MSTest.

---

Would you like me to apply this refactoring to the file?
